"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2235\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2235\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.823ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 2131.6 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D459\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D459\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(575.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1631.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>]</p>\n<p></p><p>(d) The plot of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> vs <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.02ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 451 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> for various azimuthal quantum numbers, shows peak shifting towards higher <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.02ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 451 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> value.<br>\nThis statement is typically true. The radial distribution functions of atomic orbitals (which can be related to <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.564ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.718ex\" height=\"2.451ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 1643.6 1083.4\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(278,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(929,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> vs <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.02ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 451 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) for higher azimuthal quantum numbers generally have their peaks at larger values of the radial distance <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.02ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 451 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> from the nucleus. This is because with higher <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.674ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 298 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D459\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D459\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> values, the electrons are more likely to be found at greater distances due to the higher angular momentum.</p>The given plot is<p></p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l3ofg2sv/8310697b-4c0d-473c-8a0e-02f2c197bb61/7bba7bf0-ddb9-11ec-b7c7-a7ac6d9334e0/file-1l3ofg2sw.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1l3ofg2sv/8310697b-4c0d-473c-8a0e-02f2c197bb61/7bba7bf0-ddb9-11ec-b7c7-a7ac6d9334e0/file-1l3ofg2sw.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (On